    "summary": "In an extraordinary meeting, Somalia's Council of Ministers approved a bill to amend the electoral law, a move that could reshape the country's voting system ahead of future polls.",
    "content": "Somalia's Council of Ministers convened an emergency session on Wednesday evening and approved a draft bill that would amend the country's electoral law, according to a statement from the government. The proposed legislation, which has not yet been publicly detailed, now moves to the Federal Parliament for review and potential ratification.\n\nThe meeting was chaired by Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre and included several key ministers, though the government did not disclose the specific changes contained in the bill. The expedited nature of the session suggests the administration views the amendment as time-sensitive, possibly linked to preparations for upcoming regional and national elections.\n\nThis development follows months of debate over Somalia's electoral framework, which has been criticized for its indirect clan-based model. The current system, which uses delegates to select lawmakers, has been in place since 2016 and was also employed in the 2022 elections. Various stakeholders, including opposition parties and civil society groups, have called for a transition to universal suffrage, but progress has been slow due to security and logistical challenges.\n\nThe timing of the cabinet's decision is significant, as Somalia is scheduled to hold local elections in several regions later this year, with national polls expected in 2026. The amendment could introduce changes to the electoral process, such as the adoption of a one-person, one-vote system or adjustments to the clan power-sharing formula. However, without the bill's text, the full implications remain unclear.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, which plays a vital role in the country's economy and politics through remittances and advocacy, any changes to the electoral law could affect their participation. Diaspora communities have long pushed for greater inclusion in the political process, including the right to vote from abroad. The new bill may address these concerns, but details are awaited.\n\nAs the bill proceeds to parliament, it is expected to spark further debate among lawmakers and the public. The government has not announced a timeline for the parliamentary review. This report will be updated as more information becomes available.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/xukuumadda-somalia-oo-meel-marisay-waxka-beddelka-xeerka-doorashooyinka/427972/",
